": "direct"}, "input": {"title": "payments-worker v3.4.0 unhealthy after deploy; rolled back to v3.3.0", "severity": "SEV2", "service": "payments-worker", "summary": "payments-worker v3.4.0, deployed this morning to us-east, us-west and eu-west, is unhealthy with an 18% error rate. payments-api (v5.1.0) is also showing 18% errors, which the user identified as downstream of the worker. Rolling payments-worker back to v3.3.0, its last release that was healthy in production. payments-api has not been changed.", "idempotency_key": "payments-worker-v3.4.0-rollback-sev2", "confidence": 0.92}, "name": "create_incident", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I rolled payments-worker back to v3.3.0 and opened SEV2 incident INC-0001, which paged on-call. payments-api is still showing errors, though.\n\n- **Rollback worked:** payments-worker is on v3.3.0 in us-east, us-west and eu-west. It's healthy, and its error rate dropped from 18% to 0.2%.\n- **Incident:** my first attempt to open it timed out. I checked the incident list, confirmed nothing had been created, then tried again with the same request key so it couldn't make a duplicate. You have one incident, INC-0001.\n- **payments-api has not recovered:** it's still on v5.1.0 at 18% errors. When I checked, the worker rollback had only just finished, so the API may need a few minutes to clear. If the errors don't drop, the API's own release could be causing them, separately from the worker. It's v5.1.0, marked degraded in its release history. Its last healthy release was v5.0.0.\n\nI didn't change payments-api. I can check it again in a few minutes. If it's still failing, I can roll it back to v5.0.0 too, but only if you say so.", "type": "text"}]}]}